    {
      "id": "43kmMRTNaok2y0nlbpajR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -402.9829063166918,
      "y": 1857.5382665976817,
      "width": 2574.36673358407,
      "height": 2057.7833069304534,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b58",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 705701056,
      "version": 2187,
      "versionNonce": 101228640,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787226640415,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Q3: read/watch path scaling\n\nWhy its important to optimize read operation?\nBecause youtube is read heavy\n\n\nscenario: User from US uploads a video and a user from India watches \nit explain what happens?\n\n1. What happens step by step from \"user clicks play\" to \"video starts streaming\" — where does the request \ngo first, and how does it find the video fast? \n\n- user clicks play \n- client browser requests the vidoe's manifest file from the nearest CDN edge server\n- manfiest file is like an index of indexes. Pointing to the various resolution and the chunks\n- request it from the nearest CDN edge\n- if cache hit then server directly as a result low latency\n\n\n2. What happens if the video is not yet cached in the India CDN edge (cache miss) — \nwhat's the fallback path? \ncache miss - CDN server doesn't have the original video\n- take a pull from original blob storage\n- the first users pays a latency penalty after that all user uses the cached version\n\n\n3. How does the client actually decide which resolution to request, and how does it switch mid-playback if network conditions change\n (e.g., user goes from WiFi to weak mobile data)?\n\nABR = adaptive bitrate streaming\n\nbased on the clients network strength decide which resolution to server \nwithout starting the playback from the beginning\n\n- client monitors it download throughput (how fast the recent chunks arrive)\n- each chunk is 2-10 seconds long\n- before requesting the ABR algorithm computes which resolution to send next\n- because chunks are independent file per resolutions switching is seamless \nno restart just the next chunk come from a differenet quality tier\n\n4.  how would you handle the \"view count\" update for this video without hammering your database on every single play?\n- the count are not updated every time a new viewer arrives. For viral/trending videos it can hammer the DB\nmultiple times for the same row cause serious contention\n- the count is pushed to a async message queue\n- after sometime in one go the count is updated\n- therefore two viewer can see different view count. Since it can be stale. Because \navailability is emphaise over consistency.",
      "fontSize": 35.02609884136942,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Q3: read/watch path scaling\n\nWhy its important to optimize read operation?\nBecause youtube is read heavy\n\n\nscenario: User from US uploads a video and a user from India watches \nit explain what happens?\n\n1. What happens step by step from \"user clicks play\" to \"video starts streaming\" — where does the request \ngo first, and how does it find the video fast? \n\n- user clicks play \n- client browser requests the vidoe's manifest file from the nearest CDN edge server\n- manfiest file is like an index of indexes. Pointing to the various resolution and the chunks\n- request it from the nearest CDN edge\n- if cache hit then server directly as a result low latency\n\n\n2. What happens if the video is not yet cached in the India CDN edge (cache miss) — \nwhat's the fallback path? \ncache miss - CDN server doesn't have the original video\n- take a pull from original blob storage\n- the first users pays a latency penalty after that all user uses the cached version\n\n\n3. How does the client actually decide which resolution to request, and how does it switch mid-playback if network conditions change\n (e.g., user goes from WiFi to weak mobile data)?\n\nABR = adaptive bitrate streaming\n\nbased on the clients network strength decide which resolution to server \nwithout starting the playback from the beginning\n\n- client monitors it download throughput (how fast the recent chunks arrive)\n- each chunk is 2-10 seconds long\n- before requesting the ABR algorithm computes which resolution to send next\n- because chunks are independent file per resolutions switching is seamless \nno restart just the next chunk come from a differenet quality tier\n\n4.  how would you handle the \"view count\" update for this video without hammering your database on every single play?\n- the count are not updated every time a new viewer arrives. For viral/trending videos it can hammer the DB\nmultiple times for the same row cause serious contention\n- the count is pushed to a async message queue\n- after sometime in one go the count is updated\n- therefore two viewer can see different view count. Since it can be stale. Because \navailability is emphaise over consistency.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "2bhJ__6kr6gMMSOLlE7Oe",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 72.59519993745846,
      "y": 80.2244907578792,
